WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.176
What fills the window over a long task.

2
00:00:02.456 --> 00:00:08.088
Picture the research agent from the lab, twenty steps into a question about a project’s launch risks.

3
00:00:08.368 --> 00:00:14.469
Its context window now holds: the system prompt and tool definitions, sent on every request;.

4
00:00:14.749 --> 00:00:17.160
the task, the user’s original message;.

5
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.621
every assistant turn: text, thinking blocks, and tool_use requests;.

6
00:00:21.901 --> 00:00:26.061
every tool result: search hits, document pages, error messages.

7
00:00:26.341 --> 00:00:27.707
The first two are fixed.

8
00:00:27.987 --> 00:00:40.061
The last two grow with every step, and tool results usually grow fastest: one document read can be thousands of tokens, and the model rarely needs it again after it has extracted the fact it wanted.

9
00:00:40.341 --> 00:00:52.075
The Claude Agent SDK docs make the same observation about Claude Code’s own loop: everything accumulates within a session, and large tool outputs consume significant context.

10
00:00:52.355 --> 00:00:53.955
Growth costs you in three ways.

11
00:00:54.235 --> 00:01:03.664
Money and latency, because the whole history is resent on every request (prompt caching softens this, since an unchanged prefix is cheaper to read).

12
00:01:03.944 --> 00:01:07.272
Hard limits, because even a 1M-token window ends.

13
00:01:07.552 --> 00:01:14.677
And quality, which is the subject of the next section, because a model does not use a long context as well as a short one.

14
00:01:14.957 --> 00:01:18.968
Context engineering is Anthropic’s name for managing all of this.

15
00:01:19.248 --> 00:01:33.541
Their September 2025 post defines it as curating and maintaining the whole set of tokens the model sees at each step (instructions, tools, external data, message history), not just writing the first prompt.

16
00:01:33.821 --> 00:01:40.840
The guiding principle they give is to find the smallest set of high-signal tokens that makes the desired outcome likely.
